    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cancer is a leading cause of mortality worldwide. Despite new therapeutics to battle malignancies, the uniqueness of each cancer type poses a challenge in producing broadly effective therapeutics, requiring detailed molecular and mechanistic insights to develop new treatments. My research focuses on a therapeutic strategy which targets post-translational modifications (PTMs). Specifically, lysine methylation, on eukaryotic elongation factor 1A isoform 1 (eEF1A1), an evolutionarily conserved protein essential for ribosome-catalyzed protein synthesis. Different methylation events on eEF1A have been associated with the development and progression of cancer. For example, METTL13, a lysine methyltransferase (KMT), di-methylates lysine 55 on eEF1A1 (eEF1AK55me2) to facilitate mRNA translate and thereby promote RAS-driven pancreatic and lung cancers. In this context, deletion of METTL13 and loss of eEF1AK55me2 in human and mouse tumor models inhibits RAS-driven tumors. My research aims to characterize the molecular function and role in cancer of another eEF1A KMT enzyme named N-6 Adenosine Methyltransferase 2 (N6AMT2) which tri-methylates eEF1A at lysine position 79 (eEF1AK79me3). To elucidate N6AMT2’s biological mechanism in cancer development, cell viability and surface sensing of translation (SUnSET) assays were conducted in lung adenocarcinoma (A549) cells to test if N6AMT2 knockdown reduces proliferation rates and protein synthesis rates. The CRISPR/Cas9 system was utilized for the assays to create a positive control and two knockdown N6AMT2 constructs named, respectively: N6AMT2 sgSAFE, N6AMT2 sg1, and N6AMT2 sg2, respectively. Results indicate that eEF1AK79me3 had no impact on protein synthesis rates or promotes proliferation in the chosen lung adenocarcinoma (A549) cell line. Thus, N6AMT2 does not play a role in promoting oncogenesis and cannot be a therapeutic target. In the future, my hope is that more research is dedicated to other eEF1A KMT’s that could potentially be used for generation of clinical inhibitors to treat cancer patients.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
